{"format_version":"vbulletin-3+-thread","url":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing","id":775460,"title":"Torpor memory distortion now not really a thing?","path":["Home","Onyx Path","White Wolf Worlds","Chronicles of Darkness","Vampire: The Requiem"],"posts":[{"date":"2015-11-27T07:38:16-08:00","id":775460,"title":"Torpor memory distortion now not really a thing?","content":"I just want to confirm this change, in VtR1, Torpor tended to cause vampires to dream, presumably lucidly, such that after years they might not have been sure which version of events really happened. Now this happens sometimes but it's rare?<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> means she\u2019s prepared to adapt to a new world. Sometimes, this<br /> goes awry; she wakes up a whole new person, or, rarely, with<br /> distorted memories of her past existence. </div> </div> </div>","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.pinterest.com/xenoterracide/chrod-inspirations/\" target=\"_blank\">Graphic Inspirational Photos for Chronicles of Darkness on Pinterest</a> <br /> <a href=\"https://chroniclesofdarkness.slack.com\" target=\"_blank\">Chronicles of Darkness Slack</a> (PM Me with Email for an Invite, then invite your friends)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4046-xenoterracide","name":"xenoterracide","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T08:06:58-08:00","id":775472,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>xenoterracide</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing#post775460\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">I just want to confirm this change, in VtR1, Torpor tended to cause vampires to dream, presumably lucidly, such that after years they might not have been sure which version of events really happened. Now this happens sometimes but it's rare?</div> </div> </div> </div>My impression is it is up to you. It still a thing but no so much. It is being deemphasized.<br /> Personally I think added something and helped in certain situatons. But is up to your table.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T08:36:42-08:00","id":775489,"title":"","content":"Yeah, basically it can still happen, but it's no longer the default assumption that it always happens. You can fiddle with the dials to suit your games, make it as common or as rare as you feel is appropriate.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>Going by Willow now, or Wil for short. She/Her/Hers.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/269-charlaquin","name":"Charlaquin","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar269_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"10831"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T10:00:38-08:00","id":775527,"title":"","content":"I really hated the concept of fog of eternity of the first edition. I menas, a vampire, living for centuries, and lose or confuse his memory...after the torpor he, not just lose his power, but also his knowledge.<br /> Now is more a drammatic thing. I use that after a really long time spent in torpor, at the awakening the vampire can be confused, but with time the memories come back to be clear.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T11:58:09-08:00","id":775570,"title":"","content":"Now it's a bit like, if you remember meeting Napoleon, you met Napoleon. On the other hand, I can't remember what I did last Tuesday, let alone what I did a year ago. Vampires probably have as much luck remembering the exact details of 200 years ago.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><hr /><b>Social justice vampire/freelancer | He/Him</b><br /> <br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Actual Play:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/852858-vampire-the-requiem-%E2%80%93-bloodlines-ap\" target=\"_blank\">Vampire: The Requiem \u2013 Bloodlines</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Masquiem:</b></span> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/5268-13-licks-the-curses-of-caine-in-blood-smoke\" target=\"_blank\">Curses of Caine in Requiem 2nd</a><br /> <span style=\"color:#A52A2A\"><b>Storytellers Vault: </b></span><a href=\"https://www.storytellersvault.com/browse.php?author=None%20More%20Dark%20Publishing#affiliate_id=321925\" target=\"_blank\">Author Page</a>","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/192-yossarian","name":"Yossarian","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar192_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"2297"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T12:07:21-08:00","id":775574,"title":"","content":"We'll probably be getting more information about this in upcoming books like Secrets of the Covenants or, more likely, a Thousand Years of Night.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T21:29:29-08:00","id":775787,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Yossarian</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing?p=775570#post775570\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Now it's a bit like, if you remember meeting Napoleon, you met Napoleon. On the other hand, I can't remember what I did last Tuesday, let alone what I did a year ago. Vampires probably have as much luck remembering the exact details of 200 years ago.</div> </div> </div> </div>yeah... kind of always wondered about the necessity, since vampires don't seem to be photographic memory. Human memory in general is pretty volatile, and it's pretty easy for us to remember things wrong.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/><a href=\"https://www.pinterest.com/xenoterracide/chrod-inspirations/\" target=\"_blank\">Graphic Inspirational Photos for Chronicles of Darkness on Pinterest</a> <br /> <a href=\"https://chroniclesofdarkness.slack.com\" target=\"_blank\">Chronicles of Darkness Slack</a> (PM Me with Email for an Invite, then invite your friends)","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/4046-xenoterracide","name":"xenoterracide","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar4046_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"563"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-27T22:55:33-08:00","id":775817,"title":"","content":"Yeah, the new rules seem to emphasize the fog of memory as being more at the discretion of the Storyteller. I prefer it that way.<br /> <br /> I understand why they implemented it. They wanted to avoid the old VtM trope of powerful elders who remember all the way back to the beginning of civilization and implement these centuries spanning policies of control, but the effect is that they essentially doubled down on the neutering of elder power. Blood potency is already a pretty extensive risk-reward structure for elder characters, and, as other people have already stated, memory is a fickle thing. Now any long term planning is innately going to be impeded by the natural flaws of memory and the telephone game of implementing your policy through proxy childer as you're forced to undergo periodic spans of torpor. It strikes me as a clever and thematically relevant means by which you can avoid conspiracies that stretch across eons.<br /> <br /> Still, I wouldn't be surprised to see new rules for long-term memory pop up in Secret of the Covenants. Something that ties into Humanity could be cool. Since VtR's Humanity rules take into account social isolation and characterize those with low Humanity as being drawn to their basic instincts and obsessive-compulsive passions, it would make sense to have a system in place where the clarity of your memory is directly tied to your Humanity level and possibly your touchstones. A vampire who operates as the shepherd for a sprawling Old South aristocratic clan might be able to list off the genealogy and traits of members of his family with perfect recollection but be clueless to the major historical events that caused his descendants to slide into poverty, while a music-obsessed Daeva who's life dates back to 18th century Austria might be able to remember conversations with classical composers practically word for word but might not be able to remember anything about the time before her embrace or recall the names of her coterie mates from her time in Salzburg. Something like that could really emphasize the importance of Humanity and touchstones in high power games since your very memories are contingent on fostering and maintaining relationships. It also speaks to the inherent tragedy of the game. Memory is ultimately finite, so where do you choose to dedicate that resource when you can live forever?","signature":"","edited":{"userid":8492,"user_link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","user":"dxanders","date":". 11-28-2015, 02:02 AM"},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/8492-dxanders","name":"dxanders","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Oct 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"378"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-28T03:50:32-08:00","id":775899,"title":"","content":"Yes, memory is finite, but vampire trascends the human limity. For example an elder with Blood Potency 8 can, hipotetically, have intelligence (the attribute linked to the memory) 8, so he could remember a lot more data, maybe not in details but in a general way.<br /> And if I'm not mistaken, now torpor lower the blood potency but not the attribute over the blod potency limit.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/7079-marcus","name":"Marcus","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar7079_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jul 2015"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"1119"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-29T14:18:17-08:00","id":776803,"title":"","content":"To say nothing of the fact that neural structures are an irrelevance to an ambulatory corpse- <i>however</i> a vampire's mind works, it doesn't have much truck with dopamine or neuroplasticity. I always wanted to play with rules letting vampires override their mental and physical psychosomatic limitations on a temporary basis, but considering the overlap with Disciplines and blood boosting and Attribute increase and so on it hardly seemed worth the Pandora's box of telling players that there is, in fact, no spoon.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":3}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1289-hastur00","name":"Hastur00","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1289_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"366"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-30T09:09:41-08:00","id":777180,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Marcus</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing?p=775899#post775899\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Yes, memory is finite, but vampire trascends the human limity. For example an elder with Blood Potency 8 can, hipotetically, have intelligence (the attribute linked to the memory) 8, so he could remember a lot more data, maybe not in details but in a general way.<br /> And if I'm not mistaken, now torpor lower the blood potency but not the attribute over the blod potency limit.</div> </div> </div> </div>It made a nice in game explanation why an elder remembers something wrong because the player is not a history buff.<br /> Besides the uncertain memory plays on the theme of loss of things that made him who he was.<br /> To me it adds to curse and the paranoia. Especially if you use the underworld twist and the possibility that what came out torpor wasn't old Marcus after all.","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>If you want By Night Studios to release new LARP RULES for NWoD,<br /> <a href=\"http://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/general/conventions-and-gatherings/313137-open-letter-to-by-night-studios\" target=\"_blank\">like this post</a>.<br /> <br /> <img class=\"bbcode-attachment js-need-data-att-update\" src=\"filedata/fetch?filedataid=62\" data-tempid=\"temp_62_1418088885567_853\" />","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/594-chattnos","name":"Chattnos","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar594_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"879"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-30T09:53:17-08:00","id":777210,"title":"","content":"<div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing?p=777180#post777180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">It made a nice in game explanation why an elder remembers something wrong because the player is not a history buff.</div> </div> </div> </div>Maybe that elder did go through the Fog of Ages, then. It's no longer a hard fact but evidently it does happen.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing?p=777180#post777180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">Besides the uncertain memory plays on the theme of loss of things that made him who he was.</div> </div> </div> </div>Sure, but it does so in a way that's perhaps a bit unsubtle. Losing your place and time is very disorienting for one's sense of identity, and Kindred are said to have to reinvent themselves over and over as time goes on. Imagine the psychological toll of doing that. You really don't need them to literally lose memories to suffer that sense of disorientation, though if one must, once again, you can have it so that particular elder DID suffer those side effects.<br /> <br /> <div class=\"bbcode_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote\"> <div class=\"quote_container\"> <div class=\"bbcode_quote_container b-icon b-icon__ldquo-l--gray\"></div> <div class=\"bbcode_postedby\"> Originally posted by <strong>Chattnos</strong> <a href=\"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/forum/main-category/main-forum/the-new-world-of-darkness/vampire-the-requiem/775460-torpor-memory-distortion-now-not-really-a-thing?p=777180#post777180\" rel=\"nofollow\" title=\"View Post\" class=\"b-icon b-icon__double-arrow-right--blue animate-scroll\">View Post</a> </div> <div class=\"message\">To me it adds to curse and the paranoia. Especially if you use the underworld twist and the possibility that what came out torpor wasn't old Marcus after all.</div> </div> </div> </div>None of which is invalidated by it not being the default. There's plenty of paranoia to be had when 1/5th of the entire population can re-write your memories, everybody can lie, and you can lose huge stretches of time to possession by immortal shadow creatures that hate you.<br /> <br /> Apply the <i>possibility </i>of unreliably memory via the <i>chance </i>of the Fog of Ages atop that liberally, and there's plenty of reason to be paranoid.<br /> <br /> Normal people's memories aren't sacrosanct anyway, and that's just for a decades.<br />","signature":"<hr class=\"b-divider\"/><br/>I am no longer participating in the community. Please do not contact me about my previous work.","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/159-leetsepeak","name":"Leetsepeak","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"4259"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-30T10:58:04-08:00","id":777240,"title":"","content":"I don't know about your age but if you're getting old and your teenager days are long past, you'll remember these days in a subjective way: sure you were alive and aware during these days but your memory is selective. Didn't ever happen to you (old folks as I am ^^) to meet with a childhood friend, and suddenly he has different memories, he remembers things you just didn't care to and he's casting a new light on those good old days of yore. Now imagine you're 300 or more year old, how your memories would be reliable ? In my opinion, the Fog was a blunt, rough attempt to transcript that: it's worth mentioning, I'm not sure turning it into a rule mechanics would work, but taking this into account, imo, was worthing a shot. You can play on it: elders' memories are great but they are not history books, they are not unbiased (far from it) but there's still wisdom to gain from it ; the problem is : how to separate grain from shaft ... and then guess what ? you might have another story to tell. I think they let it down in the new version because it's too complicated and impossible to translate into a system, but it's an interesting question.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1851-jps","name":"JPS","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1851_2.png","title":"Member","rank":0,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"209"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-30T12:00:12-08:00","id":777282,"title":"","content":"Fog of Ages always seemed silly to me. I get that you don't want perfect memory of the past because it ruins the mystery. Normal human memory is fallible enough for that, which only a rare few vampires (BP 5+) will ever surpass. Beyond that, vampires lie. Even if a vampire has Eidetic Memory and remembers everything 100%, they'll lie when it suits them to create a more beneficial narrative. You don't need Fog of Ages for its original purpose at all, so I'm glad they largely discarded it.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":2}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/1692-maina","name":"Maina","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar1692_2.png","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Dec 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"724"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-11-30T13:00:06-08:00","id":777312,"title":"","content":"Seems like it was one of the 'lets not be like cWoD' concepts, getting rid of the powerful elders that rule the world indiscriminately.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/886-nofather","name":"nofather","avatar":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/core/avatars/0/0/6/0/4/5/avatar886_2.jpeg","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Nov 2013"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"11016"}]},"thankedby":[]},{"date":"2015-12-01T00:57:59-08:00","id":777612,"title":"","content":"I was fond of the Fog, though it was honestly mostly because I really liked the Agonistes. One impression I get from the new edition is that they're placing more emphasis on vampires being well-adapted to their role; hence why they can now see well in the dark, scent blood like a shark, and adapt quickly after torpor. However, to me, having a supernaturally excellent memory doesn't seem like something that is necessarily an &quot;adaptive&quot; trait for immortal predator-parasites. <br /> <br /> I think part of the tragedy of vampires (in the Requiem setting, at least) is that, while it seems like their immortality would grant them wisdom and perspective, and make them incredible repositories of historical knowledge, that's not the case. Instead, elder vampires tend to be shallow, selfish, petty, and paranoid, having hidden themselves behind lies for so long that even they don't remember the truth of what they've experienced. As much as they try to rise above monsters that the curse would make them, they're simply not well equipped to be anything else.","signature":"","edited":{"userid":0,"user_link":"","user":"","date":""},"attachments":[],"reactions":[{"type":"\ud83d\udc4d","count":1}],"user":{"link":"https://forum.theonyxpath.com/member/3209-catdoom","name":"CatDoom","avatar":"data:image/svg+xml;base64,","title":"Member","rank":5,"rank-name":"","custom":[{"key":"Join Date:","value":"Jun 2014"},{"key":"Posts:","value":"370"}]},"thankedby":[]}]}